    "elevatorPitch": "We are rarely deliberate liars; instead, our brains run an automated PR department that rewrites reality to protect our fragile egos. Tavris and Aronson reveal the invisible mental gears that turn honest errors into entrenched delusions\u2014and show us how to break the cycle.",
    "executiveSummary": "Imagine driving down a highway and missing your exit. Instead of admitting the mistake and making a U-turn, your mind convinces you that the scenic route is superior, the road signs were defective, and you meant to travel this direction all along. This is cognitive dissonance in action: the psychological friction that occurs when who we believe we are (competent, moral, sensible) collides with what we actually did (a blunder, an injustice, a poor decision). To extinguish that burning discomfort, our minds reflexively manufacture rationalizations, transforming honest slip-ups into rigid convictions.\n\nThe authors model this progression through the vivid architecture of the 'Pyramid of Choice.' Picture two hikers starting at the peak of a mountain with nearly identical viewpoints. Confronted with a subtle fork in the path, each takes one modest step in an opposite direction. To justify that minor stride, each hiker minimizes counterarguments and exaggerates the virtues of their chosen path. Step by small step, they descend into separate valleys. By the time they reach the bottom, they stand miles apart in entrenched, hostile camps\u2014convinced the other side is foolish or corrupt, entirely blind to the identical start of their journeys.\n\nThis self-protective loop quietly sabotages every domain of adulthood. It operates in marriages when kitchen-sink disagreements calcify into contempt, in boardrooms when leaders double down on failing capital projects, and in courthouses when investigators overlook exculpatory evidence to preserve an early hunch. Recognizing that memory behaves not like a permanent video recording, but like a self-serving historian rewriting past archives, is the first step toward intellectual maturity. Growth requires cultivating the rare stamina to separate error from personal worth.",
