      "headline": "The Blind Spot in Your Rearview Mirror",
      "keyInsight": "Everyone believes they see the world in high definition. We think our eyes take pure photos of pure reality. That is why disagreement feels baffling. If you see clearly and someone disagrees, you assume they are biased, blind, or corrupt! This is the ultimate blind spot. We easily spot self-interest in our neighbor. When a rival salesman praises a product, we notice the commission. When we praise our own work, we swear it is pure honesty. Our brains hide our own bias behind tinted glass while shining floodlights on everyone else. The smarter you think you are, the deeper your blind spot grows.",
      "content": "Picture driving down a busy freeway on a rainy afternoon. You check your side mirrors before switching lanes. No matter how clean your mirrors are, every car has a blind spot. A huge truck can sit right next to your rear bumper, completely invisible. Our minds carry the exact same design flaw. We clearly see everyone else cutting lanes and swerving. Yet we remain totally blind to our own wobbles.\n\nThis flaw creates the illusion of personal purity. When a judge receives free tickets to a football game from a friend, she feels insulted if anyone questions her fairness. She genuinely believes her judgment remains untainted. Meanwhile, if an opposing judge accepted the same tickets, she would suspect bribery instantly. We judge ourselves by our pure inner intentions. We judge other people strictly by their outward actions.\n\nThis double standard poisons discussions at the office and dinner table alike. We assume our team operates on honest facts while the other team runs on selfish prejudice. We bring magnifying glasses to inspect their flaws. Yet we wear blindfolds when looking at our own motives. Humility begins when you realize your mirror has the exact same blind angle as everyone else.",
