      "headline": "Memory: The Creative Storyteller in Your Head",
      "keyInsight": "Most people treat memory like a digital camcorder. You press record, file the tape, and replay the footage later. But human memory works more like an eager personal biographer. It edits the script every single time you tell the story! If you made a foolish investment years ago, your memory trims out your greed. Instead, it highlights how external market storms tricked you. If a relationship ended badly, your memory casts you as the patient saint. Memory does not exist to preserve exact history. It exists to protect your self-esteem and make you look like the hero of your own life movie.",
      "content": "Think about looking through an old family recipe box. Some index cards have grease stains, and handwritten notes run along the margins. Over the decades, cooks crossed out ingredients and added spices. Memory treats your past the exact same way. It does not play back fixed files. Every time you recall an event, your brain rewires the dish to match your current taste.\n\nThis explains why two siblings can remember the exact same summer vacation in completely different ways. One recalls a joyful road trip full of laughter and ice cream. The other remembers endless flat tires and screaming arguments. Neither sibling is lying on purpose. Both brains simply adjusted the lighting to support their present feelings. When our current opinions shift, our memories quietly rewrite the backstory to match.\n\nEven worse, our minds can manufacture vivid memories out of thin air. Suggest a few believable details, and someone will swear they got lost in a shopping mall as a toddler. They will describe the security guard and the color of the candy counter with tears in their eyes, even if it never happened. Confident memory is not proof of truth. It is simply proof that your inner storyteller did a convincing editing job.",
