      "headline": "The Doctor's Blind Spot",
      "keyInsight": "Here is a scary secret about experts. The more confident they sound, the more blind they might be. Imagine a mechanic who thinks every engine noise is a broken water pump. If you tell him the radiator works fine, he just smiles. He assumes you do not hear what he hears. Therapists and clinicians often fall into this exact trap. They form a theory about a person in five minutes. After that, every single thing you say becomes proof they were right. If you agree with their diagnosis, you confirm their genius. If you deny it, they claim you are just in deep denial. There is no way for them to lose. That sounds comforting for the doctor's ego, but it is dangerous for the patient. True science requires testing yourself against cold facts. Real pros look for clues that prove their pet hunch completely wrong.",
      "content": "Imagine you are baking sourdough bread for the first time. You pull out a flat, heavy loaf from the oven. You immediately blame the room temperature. You tell yourself the yeast was too cold to wake up. You do not check the expiration date on the jar. You do not weigh your flour. You simply pick an answer that makes you feel competent and move on. Professionals do this with human minds every single day.\n\nClinicians often trust their gut over systematic lab tests. A gut feeling feels intimate, warm, and wise. Standardized charts feel cold, robotic, and distant. Yet statistical formulas beat human hunches almost every time in head-to-head trials. When a counselor relies purely on intuition, they build an echo chamber. Every question they ask nudges the client toward a pre-set story.\n\nConsider the wild panic around repressed memories in the late twentieth century. Well-meaning therapists used guided imagery like a metal detector on wet sand. They were convinced trauma was buried beneath every symptom. If a client remembered a happy childhood, the therapist suspected a cover-up. The helper ended up implanting false memories rather than uncovering true history. Their good intentions paved a highway straight into fiction.\n\nReal expertise is not about having unbreakable confidence in your first guess. It is about actively trying to shoot down your favorite idea. Think of a careful ship captain checking radar against the dark horizon. He does not just look for clear water; he actively scans for hidden rocks. When we stop checking our instruments, our intuition turns into pure superstition.",
