      "headline": "Cops, Courts, and Tunnel Vision",
      "keyInsight": "We love to believe the justice system operates like a digital scale. In reality, it works more like a speeding freight train. Once detectives pick a suspect, the tracks lock in place. Every scrap of paper must point toward that single target. If fingerprints do not match, investigators assume the suspect wore gloves. If an alibi checks out, they assume the witness is lying. The wild part is that police officers rarely do this out of malice. They genuinely believe they are putting a monster behind bars. Admitting an error means admitting an innocent soul sat in prison while the real criminal walked free. That thought is so sickening to the soul that the brain invents wild mental gymnastics to avoid facing it.",
      "content": "Picture driving down a dark country road in a thick fog. Your headlights catch a wooden post by the ditch. For a split second, your brain thinks it is a deer. Once you think deer, every shadow nearby looks like antlers and hooves. You slow down, heart pounding, totally convinced. Police interrogation rooms run on that exact same brand of tunnel vision.\n\nDetectives often decide who is guilty long before formal charges are filed. From that instant forward, the job shifts from solving a mystery to winning a case. Investigators lean hard on suspects during grueling multi-hour interrogations. They promise lenient treatment if the person just cooperates. Exhausted, terrified people will eventually say whatever ends the interrogation. An innocent person signs a confession simply to make the room stop spinning.\n\nEven after DNA tests prove absolute innocence, prosecutors often dig in their heels. They claim the freed prisoner was still an accomplice. Or they claim the lab technicians botched the swab. Changing your mind after ten years feels like tearing down your own moral house. It means accepting that your mistake stole a decade of someone's life.\n\nTrue justice demands the discipline of an airplane flight inspector. An inspector never assumes an engine is sound just because yesterday's flight landed safely. They examine the loose bolts, look for hairline cracks, and follow the physical evidence wherever it leads.",
