      "headline": "Wounds, Rifts, and Wars",
      "keyInsight": "Here is the wildest trick human groups pull on themselves. When two rival sides clash, each side remembers only the punches they received. Neither side remembers throwing the first punch. Both sides believe they are peaceful gardeners who only picked up stones in self-defense. Over time, each group rewrites history to look purely noble. You see your own group as generous, calm, and forgiving. You see the other group as cruel, sneaky, and mean. If they make a peace offer, you think it is a trap. If you offer a truce, you think you are being generous. This creates a terrible seesaw where both sides keep retaliating for past hurts. The secret trap is that nobody feels like an attacker. Everyone genuinely believes they are just standing their ground. When you think you are pure, cruelty starts to feel like justice.",
      "content": "Imagine two neighbors sharing a wooden fence. One neighbor trims a tree branch that hangs over the driveway. The other neighbor sees this as an act of war. Instead of talking, he retaliates by mowing over the flowerbed. Now both neighbors feel deeply wronged. Each person tells their family a tale of total innocence. In their own story, they were attacked without reason. This is how small spats turn into bitter neighborhood feuds and how quiet tensions turn into international wars.\n\nGroups run on loyalty, but loyalty has a sneaky blind spot. When you join a team, you put on tinted glasses. Everything your side does looks sensible and fair. Everything the rival group does looks suspicious and cruel. If your team drops litter, you say the wind blew it away. If the other team drops a wrapper, you call them messy and rude. We judge our friends by their good intentions. We judge our enemies only by their worst actions.\n\nThis pattern traps people in an endless spiral of payback. Each punch is seen as payback for the last punch. Because neither side remembers starting the fight, both sides feel entitled to revenge. You cannot fix a leaking pipe if both plumbers insist the other broke it. Breaking the cycle means someone has to put down the megaphone. True peace begins only when both sides admit their own hands are dirty.",
